<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">JCAT-25-54</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/RCT.0000000000001769</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="art-access-id">00006</article-id><article-version article-version-type="Version of Record" vocab="JAV" vocab-identifier="http://www.niso.org/publications/rp/RP-8-2008.pdf">3</article-version><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Abdominopelvic Imaging: Gastrointestinal</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Heterogeneity Habitats -Derived Radiomics of Gd-EOB-DTPA Enhanced MRI for Predicting Proliferation of Hepatocellular Carcinoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid" authenticated="false">https://orcid.org/0009-0008-4234-9848</contrib-id><name><surname>Sun</surname><given-names>Shifang</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">*</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">&#x02020;</xref><email>ssffyf1124@163.com</email></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>Yixing</given-names></name><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">&#x02021;</xref><email>949601028@qq.com</email></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Xiao</surname><given-names>Shungen</given-names></name><xref rid="aff4" ref-type="aff">&#x000a7;</xref><email>952801200@qq.com</email></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>He</surname><given-names>Qi</given-names></name><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">&#x02020;</xref><email>893628467@qq.com</email></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Jiang</surname><given-names>Zhen</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">*</xref><email>jiangzhen_sd1@126.com</email></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Fan</surname><given-names>Yanfen</given-names></name><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">&#x02021;</xref><email>fanyanfensu1@126.com</email></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1">
<label>*</label>School of Mechatronic Engineering and Automation, Shanghai University, Shanghai, China</aff><aff id="aff2">
<label>&#x02020;</label>Zhejiang International Maritime College, Zhoushan, China</aff><aff id="aff3">
<label>&#x02021;</label>Department of Radiology, The First Affiliated Hospital of Soochow University, Suzhou, China</aff><aff id="aff4">
<label>&#x000a7;</label>College of Information and Mechanical &#x00026;Electrical Engineering, Ningde Normal University, Ningde, China</aff><author-notes><corresp>Correspondence to: Yanfen Fan, Department of Radiology, The First Affiliated Hospital of Soochow University, Shizi Street 188, Suzhou 215006, Jiangsu, People&#x02019;s Republic of China (e-mail:<email>fanyanfensu1@126.com</email>); Zhen Jiang, School of Mechatronic Engineering and Automation, Shanghai University, Shangda Road 99, Baoshan District, Shanghai, People&#x02019;s Republic of China (e-mail: <email>jiangzhen_sd1@126.com</email>).</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Nov-Dec</season><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>2</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>49</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>880</fpage><lpage>890</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>10</day><month>2</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>22</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). Published by Wolters Kluwer Health, Inc.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives License 4.0 (CCBY-NC-ND)</ext-link>, where it is permissible to download and share the work provided it is properly cited. The work cannot be changed in any way or used commercially without permission from the journal.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="rct-49-880.pdf"/><abstract><sec><title>Objective:</title><p>To construct and validate the optimal model for preoperative prediction of proliferative HCC based on habitat-derived radiomics features of Gd-EOB-DTPA-Enhanced MRI.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods:</title><p>A total of 187 patients who underwent Gd-EOB-DTPA-enhanced MRI before curative partial hepatectomy were divided into training (n=130, 50 proliferative and 80 nonproliferative HCC) and validation cohort (n=57, 25 proliferative and 32 nonproliferative HCC). Habitat subregion generation was performed using the Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) clustering method to cluster all pixels to identify similar subregions within the tumor. Radiomic features were extracted from each tumor subregion in the arterial phase (AP) and hepatobiliary phase (HBP). Independent sample <italic toggle="yes">t</italic> tests, Pearson correlation coefficient, and Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (LASSO) algorithm were performed to select the optimal features of subregions. After feature integration and selection, machine-learning classification models using the sci-kit-learn library were constructed. Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves and the DeLong test were performed to compare the identified performance for predicting proliferative HCC among these models.</p></sec><sec><title>Results:</title><p>The optimal number of clusters was determined to be 3 based on the Silhouette coefficient. 20, 12, and 23 features were retained from the AP, HBP, and the combined AP and HBP habitat (subregions 1, 2, 3) radiomics features. Three models were constructed with these selected features in AP, HBP, and the combined AP and HBP habitat radiomics features. The ROC analysis and DeLong test show that the Naive Bayes model of AP and HBP habitat radiomics (AP-HBP-Hab-Rad) archived the best performance. Finally, the combined model using the Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM) algorithm, incorporating the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, age, and AFP (Alpha-Fetoprotein), was identified as the optimal model for predicting proliferative HCC. For the training and validation cohort, the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and AUC were 0.923, 0.880, 0.950, 0.966 (95% CI: 0.937-0.994) and 0.825, 0.680, 0.937, 0.877 (95% CI: 0.786-0.969), respectively. In its validation cohort of the combined model, the AUC value was statistically higher than the other models (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.01).</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions:</title><p>A combined model, including AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, serum AFP, and age using the LightGBM algorithm, can satisfactorily predict proliferative HCC preoperatively.</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical"><p>
<fig position="float" id="GA1"><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g001" position="float"/></fig>
</p></abstract><kwd-group><title>Key Words:</title><kwd>hepatocellular carcinoma</kwd><kwd>GD-EOB-DTPA</kwd><kwd>habitats</kwd><kwd>radiomics</kwd><kwd>proliferation</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group id="ID0EN4AE4777"><funding-source>.Jiangsu Province Capability Improvement Proiect through Science, Technology and Education(Jiangsu ProvinciaL Medical Key Discipline Cultivation Unit)</funding-source><award-id>JSDW202242</award-id><principal-award-recipient>Yanfen Fan</principal-award-recipient></award-group><award-group id="ID0ESAAG4778"><funding-source>Suzhou Key Laboratory of Medical lmaging</funding-source><award-id>SZS2024032</award-id><principal-award-recipient>Yanfen Fan</principal-award-recipient></award-group></funding-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>OPEN-ACCESS</meta-name><meta-value>TRUE</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><p>Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the sixth most common cancer and the third leading cause of cancer-related death worldwide.<sup><xref rid="R1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup> It is a difficult-to-treat cancer, as it exhibits well-known high heterogeneity at the molecular and histologic levels. Even in the same HCC, there are some different subregions. Different subregions have unique structural, metabolic, and functional characteristics.<sup><xref rid="R2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref></sup> HCC heterogeneity introduces challenges in clinical treatment decision-making and follow-up management.</p><p>Recently, Calderaro et al<sup><xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup> proposed an integrated classification of HCC, the proliferative and nonproliferative phenotype. Proliferative HCC is associated with aggressive behavior, which is characterized by the activation of varied genomic pathways related to cellular proliferation and survival.<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref></sup> Therefore, predicting the aggressive HCC subtypes before treatment may have pivotal prognostic and therapeutic implications. Histologically, proliferative HCCs comprise the positive expression of cytokeratin 19 (CK-19), positive expression of cytokeratin 7 (CK-7), macrotrabecular (MTM), scirrhous, sarcomatoid, and neutrophil-rich types,<sup><xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref></sup> whereas steatohepatitic, clear cell, lymphocyte-rich, CK19-negative and CK7-negative conventional HCCs were designated nonproliferative HCCs. Previous reports have indicated that CT and MRI imaging features of HCC were correlated to proliferative HCC.<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup>
</p><p>Gadolinium ethoxybenzyl-diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid (Gd-EOB-DTPA) enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), which has intracellular and extracellular contrast characteristics, could reflect molecular and pathologic factors of HCCs.<sup><xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> Previous studies have demonstrated that HCCs with signal heterogeneity in the HBP of EOB-MRI showed more malignant behavior compared with other HCCs.<sup><xref rid="R11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="R12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup>
</p><p>Previous studies have shown tumors have different subregions, also known as habitats.<sup><xref rid="R13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup> The heterogeneity of tumors&#x02019; habitat represents the microenvironment of tumor cells. In addition, radiomics has been a surrogate mechanism for noninvasively reflecting tumor microenvironment. For HCC, these tumor habitats include but are not limited to tumor neovascularity shown in the AP and tumor cellular function in the hepatobiliary phase (HBP). Recently, MRI-based quantification of intratumoral heterogeneity for predicting treatment response and prognosis in breast cancer and glioblastoma has achieved good performance.<sup><xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref></sup> Based on the HCC habitats, previous studies have predicted the recurrence, microvascular invasion, and aggression of HCC and achieved good performance.<sup><xref rid="R17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref></sup> Accordingly, we speculate that using the tumor habitats-derived radiomics features of Gd-EOB-DTPA-enhanced MRI could predict proliferative HCC.</p><sec sec-type="methods"><title>MATERIALS AND METHODS</title><sec sec-type="subjects"><title>Patients</title><p>This retrospective study was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of ****** University. According to the requirements of the IRB, informed consent from patients was waived. This study was under the ethical standards of the committee responsible for human experimentation, with the Helsinki Declaration of 1975 revised in 2013. From January 2016 to December 2023, patients who underwent Gd-EOB-DTPA-enhanced MRI before surgery and were postoperative pathologically diagnosed as HCC were consecutively enrolled in this study. The inclusion criteria were (1) pathologically confirmed HCC with immunochemical staining for CK-19, CK-7; (2) received a Gd-EOB-DTPA-enhanced MRI of the liver within 30 days before surgery. The exclusion criteria were the following: (1) have received previous treatment, such as radiofrequency ablation, antitumor therapies, transcatheter arterial chemoembolization, and so on; (2) poor image quality not meeting the diagnostic requirements; (3) the interval &#x0003e; 30 days between MRI examination and surgery; (4) incomplete pathologic or clinical information (Fig. <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). All included patients were randomly divided into training and validation cohorts at a ratio of 7:3.</p><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Inclusion and exclusion criteria. CK7 indicates cytokeratin 7; CK19, cytokeratin 19; Gd-EOB-DTPA, gadoliniumethoxybenzyl-diethylenetriamine pentaacetic acid; HCC, hepatocellular carcinoma.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g002" position="float"/></fig></sec><sec><title>Histopathologic Analyses</title><p>According to the previous study, CK-19, CK7-positive expression was defined as the presence of immunoreactivity in &#x02265;5% of the tumor to minimize false-positive findings.<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> The MTM was defined as a major trabecular structural pattern &#x0003e;50% of the tumor.<sup><xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup> CK19-positive and CK7-positive, MTM, neutrophil-rich, sclerosing, and sarcomatous HCCs were classified as proliferative HCCs; CK19-negative and CK7-negative, steatohepatitis, lymphocyte-rich, and clear-cell HCCs were classified as nonproliferative HCCs.<sup><xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>,<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref></sup>
</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>MRI Procedure</title><p>All MR images were performed using a 3.0-T MRI scanner (Magnetom Verio; Siemens Healthcare, Erlangen, Germany) with a 16-channel phase-array coil covering the whole liver. The MR acquisition sequence included (1) 3-dimensional volume interpolated breath-hold examination (3D VIBE) T1-weighted in-phase and out-phase axial imaging; (2) a respiration-triggered turbo spin-echo (TSE) T2-weighted fat-saturation (FS) axial and coronal imaging; (3) a free-breathing single-shot echo-planar diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI, b=50, 800&#x000a0;s/mm<sup>2</sup>) axial imaging; (4) the dynamic enhancement was also performed with the 3D VIBE T1-weighted FS sequence axial and coronal imaging.</p><p>All patients were required to undergo a food fast of over 6 hours and a water fast of more than 4 hours before the scanning procedure. A rapid bolus dose of Gd-EOB-DTPA (Primovist, Bayer Schering Pharma, AG, Berlin, Germany) was administered at a concentration of 25&#x000a0;&#x003bc;mol/kg of body weight. This was immediately followed by a 30&#x000a0;mL saline flush delivered at a rate of 1&#x000a0;mL/s. Imaging for the arterial phase (AP), portal venous phase (PVP), and transitional phase (TP) was conducted during suspended respiration at intervals of 30 to 35 seconds, 65 to 70 seconds, and 180 to 185 seconds, respectively. The HBP images were obtained 20 minutes after the injection of Gd-EOB-DTPA. AP and HBP images were obtained from a picture archiving and communication system (PACS, Neusoft Version 5.5).</p></sec><sec><title>Tumor Segmentation and Habitat Subregion Generation</title><p>Tumor segmentation was performed using the 3D Slicer software (version 5.2.2, <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.slicer.org" ext-link-type="uri">https://www.slicer.org</ext-link>). A radiologist (Y.Y.) with 10 years of experience manually delineated the boundaries of the target lesions layer by layer to segment the regions of interest, while another radiologist (F.Y.) with 14 years of experience reviewed the delineation results. Two radiologists would discuss and reach a consensus if there were bias labels. When the bias labels could not reach a consensus, the opinion of the senior radiologist will be followed. Before generating subregions, locating the tumor within the MR images was necessary. Each MR image was paired with a tumor mask of the same shape. We obtained the bounding box of the tumor area from the mask file to extract the tumor region. Subsequently, we extracted local radiomic information within the tumor. For each pixel inside the tumor, we centered on that pixel and extended 2 pixels in every direction, resulting in a 5 &#x000d7; 5 &#x000d7; 5 sliding window, and used Pyradiomics to extract radiomic features within this window. All features were scaled to a range of 0 to 1 for downstream clustering analysis.</p><p>Although larger windows and the extraction of more radiomic features can increase noise resistance, the computational load would grow exponentially with each additional pixel or radiomic feature within the window due to the necessity of extracting features for every pixel inside the tumor. Therefore, in this study, we cohort the number of radiomic features to 5, all derived from the Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM). These features are Contrast, DifferenceEntropy, JointEnergy, JointEntropy, and Correlation.</p><p>Ultimately, the local radiomic information of each pixel was transformed into a 5-dimensional feature vector. Pixels with similar radiomic features suggest a relative homogeneity of the tissue. Therefore, we applied the Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) clustering method to cluster all pixels to identify similar subregions within the tumor, where the number of clusters k controls the resolution of clustering. Clustering was applied at the cohort level rather than at the patient level to ensure that cluster assignments remained consistent across patients. Cluster centers derived from the training cohort were propagated to the validation cohort to ensure the same clustering was applied. To select the optimal number of clusters (ie, habitats), the Silhouette coefficient was used to evaluate the clustering results, with the k value ranging from 2 to 9. After clustering, each cluster was assigned a different color label to generate a clustered label map, reflecting the global distribution pattern of internal areas within the tumor.</p></sec><sec><title>Radiomic Feature Extraction</title><p>Radiomic features were extracted from each subregion of the tumor in both the arterial and hepatobiliary phases. Before feature extraction, MR images were preprocessed. All MR images and corresponding segmentation data were normalized by resampling to a pixel size 3 &#x000d7; 3 &#x000d7; 3&#x000a0;mm<sup>3</sup> and discretized into 25 bin widths. Eight types of filters were applied: Wavelet, Laplacian of Gaussian (LoG), Gradient (returning the magnitude of the local Gradient), Local Binary Pattern in 3D (LocalBinaryPattern3D), Exponential, Square, Square Root, and Logarithm. The open-source Python package Pyradiomics (<ext-link xlink:href="https://pyradiomics.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html" ext-link-type="uri">https://pyradiomics.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html</ext-link>, version 3.0.1, Computational Imaging and Bioinformatics Lab, Harvard Medical School) was used to extract both unfiltered (original image) and filtered features for analysis.</p><p>A total of 1197 features were extracted and categorized into the following feature classes: First Order features (234), Shape features (14), Gray Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM) features (286), Gray Level Size Zone Matrix (GLSZM) features (208), Gray Level Run Length Matrix (GLRLM) features (208), Neighboring Gray Tone Difference Matrix (NGTDM) features (65), and Gray Level Dependence Matrix (GLDM) features (182). Pyradiomics adheres to the Imaging Biomarker Standardization Initiative (IBSI). Features extracted from the training and validation cohorts were standardized using Z-score normalization.</p></sec><sec><title>Feature Selection and Model Construction</title><p>First, to select radiomic features with good reproducibility and low redundancy, we conducted independent sample <italic toggle="yes">t</italic> tests on the radiomic features, removing those with <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003e;0.05. Second, we calculated the Pearson correlation coefficient for features with high repeatability to express the relationship between features, retaining one feature from any pair with a correlation coefficient greater than 0.9. Lastly, we employed the Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (LASSO) algorithm, constructing a penalty function &#x003bb; to shrink some regression coefficients to zero, thereby incorporating stable radiomic features into the LASSO regression analysis. Based on the criterion of minimality, 10-fold cross-validation was performed to determine the optimal &#x003bb; value. The model corresponding to the optimal &#x003bb; value was used to select radiomic features with non-zero coefficients, thus obtaining independent and stable radiomic features. All selected features were standardized using the Z-score method, calculating the mean and variance for each feature column. Each feature column was transformed into a standard normal distribution by subtracting the mean and dividing by the variance. Finally, based on the features and their coefficients selected by the LASSO regression algorithm, a bar chart of feature coefficients was drawn to assess the importance of each feature. After feature integration and selection, we constructed machine-learning classification models using the sci-kit-learn library. The machine learning classification models included Logistic Regression (LR), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Decision Tree (DT), Random Forest (RF), Extremely Randomized Trees (ExtraTree), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Multilayer Perceptron (MLP), Naive Bayes, and Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM). Five-fold cross-validation was conducted to reduce overfitting and select the optimal parameters for the classification models in the training sequence. Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves were plotted, and the Area Under the Curve (AUC) was calculated.</p><p>Comparing the results of modeling with radiomic features from the AP, HBP, and the integration of both, which was AP-HBP Habitat Radiomic Signature (AP-HBP-Hab-Rad). Univariate and multivariate Logistic regression analyses were used to select risk factors related to the pathologic type. Features with <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.3 and the Habitat.</p></sec></sec><sec><title>STATISTICAL ANALYSIS</title><p>All statistical analyses were conducted using R (version 4.0.2; <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.r-project.org" ext-link-type="uri">https://www.r-project.org</ext-link>) and Python (version 3.7.2; <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.python.org" ext-link-type="uri">https://www.python.org</ext-link>). Continuous variables are presented as mean&#x000b1;SD, while categorical variables are presented using counts (n) and percentages (%). The Student <italic toggle="yes">t</italic> test was used to analyze quantitative data with a normal distribution, and Levene test was used to assess the homogeneity of variances. The Mann-Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test was used for data that were not normally distributed. The &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test was employed to compare categorical data. All statistical tests were two-sided, and statistical significance was cohort at <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05. The DeLong test was applied to compare the ROC performance between different models. The Hosmer-Lemeshow test is used to test the goodness of fit of models.</p></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>RESULTS</title><sec sec-type="subjects"><title>Patient Baseline Characteristics</title><p>A total of 187 patients were included in the study. The demographic and clinical characteristics of the patient cohort are presented in Table <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">1</xref>. The proliferative HCCs were classified as CK7-positive (31.55%, 59/187), CK19-positive (22.99%, 43/187), CK7-positive and CK19-positive (12.30%, 23/187), MTM (33.69%, 63/187-including CK7-positive or CK19-positive), neutrophil-rich (0.53%, 1/187), scirrhous (1.06, 2/187), and sarcomatoid (0%, 0/187). The remaining HCCs were nonproliferative HCCs, including CK7-negative and CK19-negative (52.94%, 99/187), clear cell (1.06%, 2/187), steatohepatitis (0.53%, 1/187), and lymphocyte-rich (0%, 0/187). After selection through univariate logistic regression with a criterion of <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.3, the clinically relevant risk factors identified were AFP and age. The results of the univariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses are shown in Table <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">2</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Clinical Characteristics of the Patients in the Training and Validation Cohorts</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1"/><th align="center" colspan="3" axis="1" rowspan="1">Training Cohort</th><th align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1"/><th align="center" colspan="3" axis="1" rowspan="1">Validation Cohort</th><th align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1"/></tr><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Characteristics</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Total</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Nonproliferative</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Proliferative</th><th align="left" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
