107)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.824</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">ALT</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.992 (0.867, 1.134)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.919</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Age</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.996 (0.990, 1.001)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.146</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.998 (0.995, 1)</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.131</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Sex</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">1.044 (0.912, 1.194)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.601</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AFP</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">1.127 (0.994, 1.278)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.116</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">1.094 (1.024, 1.169)</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.025</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">2.835 (2.622, 3.068)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">2.818 (2.609, 3.043)</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>AP-HBP-Hab-Rad Arterial phase-Hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.3 as the cutoff value to include clinical factors.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>AFP indicates alpha-fetoprotein; ALT, alanine aminotransferase; AST, aspartate aminotransferase; GGT, gamma-glutamyl transferase; HBV, hepatitis B virus; HCV, hepatitis C virus; OR, odds ratio.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Identified Subregions</title><p>The optimal number of clusters was determined to be three based on the Silhouette coefficient (Fig. <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>).</p><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Silhouette Coefficient plots to determine the optimal number of subregions (habitats). The red dotted line is the optimal value beyond which the scores started to decrease. The optimal number of clusters was determined as 3.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g003" position="float"/></fig></sec><sec><title>Feature Selection</title><p>Dimensionality reduction of AP, HBP, and the integration of AP and HBP habitat radiomics features was conducted using LASSO regression analysis. Ultimately, 20, 12, and 13 features were retained from the AP, HBP and the integration of AP and HBP habitat radiomics features, respectively (Figs. <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>).</p><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>The histogram of the selected habitat radiomics features in the AP model. The <italic toggle="yes">y</italic>-axis indicates the selected 20 features, and the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis represents the coefficient of AP habitat radiomics.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g004" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>The histogram of the selected habitat radiomics features in the HBP model. The <italic toggle="yes">y</italic>-axis indicates the selected 12 features, and the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis represents the coefficient of HBP habitat radiomics.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g005" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>FIGURE 5</label><caption><p>The histogram of the selected habitat radiomics features in the combined model. The <italic toggle="yes">y</italic>-axis indicates the selected 13 features, and the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis represents the coefficient of AP and HBP habitat radiomics.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g006" position="float"/></fig></sec><sec><title>Model Performance and Validation</title><p>In the AP habitat radiomics, the optimal model was identified as the logistic regression (LR) model. For the training cohort, the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and AUC were 0.762, 0.654, 0.833, and 0.782 (95% CI: 0.701-0.863), respectively, while for the validation cohort, they were 0.667, 0.739, 0.618, and 0.725 (95% CI: 0.592-0.858), respectively (Table <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>Performance of Different Habitat Radiomics Models for Predicting the Proliferative HCC</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1"/><th align="center" colspan="4" axis="1" rowspan="1">Training Cohort</th><th align="center" colspan="4" axis="1" rowspan="1">Validation Cohort</th></tr><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Model names</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Accuracy</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">AUC (95% CI)</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Sensitivity</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Specificity</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Accuracy</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">AUC (95% CI)</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Sensitivity</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Specificity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.762</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.782 (0.701, 0.863)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.654</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.833</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.667</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.725 (0.592, 0.858)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.739</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.618</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.677</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.693 (0.598, 0.787)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.558</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.756</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.684</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.692 (0.552, 0.832)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.435</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.853</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.923</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.966 (0.937, 0.994)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.880</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.950</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.825</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.877 (0.786, 0.969)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.680</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.937</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Combined</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.838</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.936 (0.897, 0.974)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.920</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.787</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.825</td><td align="char" char="(" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.886 (0.784, 0.988)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.760</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.875</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>AFP indicates alpha-fetoprotein; AP-HabRad, arterial phase habitat radiomic signature; AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, the integration of arterial phase and hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature; AUC, area under curve; Combined, combining the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model with age and AFP; HBP-Hab-Rad, hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>In the HBP habitat radiomics, the optimal model was also the LR model. For the training cohort, the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and AUC were 0.677, 0.558, 0.756, and 0.693 (95% CI: 0.598-0.787), respectively, while for the validation cohort, they were 0.684, 0.435, 0.853, and 0.692 (95% CI: 0.552-0.832), respectively (Table <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p><p>Among the models for the integration of arterial and hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomics, the Naive Bayes model showed the best performance. For the training cohort, the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and AUC were 0.923, 0.88, 0.950, and 0.966 (95% CI: 0.937-0.994), respectively, while for the validation cohort, they were 0.825, 0.68, 0.937, and 0.877 (95% CI: 0.786-0.969), respectively (Table <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p><p>Among these 3 models, the Naive Bayes model for the integration of AP and HBP habitat radiomics was identified as the optimal model, using its probability output as a habitat radiomics label. The optimal model, combining the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model with age and AFP, was the LightGBM model, referred to as the combined model. For the training cohort, the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and AUC of this combined model were 0.838, 0.92, 0.787, and 0.936 (95% CI: 0.897-0.974), respectively, while for the validation cohort, they were 0.825, 0.76, 0.875, and 0.886 (95% CI: 0.784-0.988), respectively (Table <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p><p>Among all models, the combined model (LightGBM algorithm) was the best, with its validation cohort AUC being higher than that of the other models, and the difference in AUC was statistically significant with a <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> value of 0.006 (Table <xref rid="T4" ref-type="table">4</xref>, Fig. <xref rid="F6" ref-type="fig">6</xref>). Calibration curves indicated good consistency between the predicted and actual outcomes in each model&#x02019;s training and validation cohorts (Fig. <xref rid="F7" ref-type="fig">7</xref>), with Hosmer-Lemeshow test <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values all &#x0003e;0.05 (Table <xref rid="T5" ref-type="table">5</xref>). Decision curve analysis (DCA) comparing the training and validation cohorts showed that the combined and AP-HBP-Hab-Rad models provided a net benefit at most threshold probabilities (Fig. <xref rid="F8" ref-type="fig">8</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T4"><label>TABLE 4</label><caption><p>Comparison of the Predictive Performance of the Different Habitat Radiomics Models in Predicting the Proliferative HCC</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Model name</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">z</italic>
</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Task</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001 (4.10919265e-16)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">8.135</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001 (5.45327692e-07)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">5.100</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">HBP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001 (5.03613713e-09))</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">5.846</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">HBP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.002</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">3.041</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001 (3.29020682e-12)</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">6.965</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad vs. combined</td><td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.006</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">2.763</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>AFP indicates alpha-fetoprotein (explanatory legends); AP-Hab-Rad, arterial phase habitat radiomic signature; AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, the integration of arterial phase and hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature; Combined, combining the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model with age and AFP; HBP-Hab-Rad, hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>FIGURE 6</label><caption><p>ROC curve of model (A) training cohort and (B) validation cohort.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g007" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>FIGURE 7</label><caption><p>Calibration curves (A) training cohort (B) validation cohort. The model demonstrates good consistency between predicted and actual outcomes, with Hosmer-Lemeshow test <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values all above 0.05. The dashed line represents the perfect prediction of an ideal model, while the colored solid line indicates the model&#x02019;s performance. The closer these 2 lines are, the better the consistency between the predicted and actual outcomes, indicating a stronger predictive capability.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g008" position="float"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T5"><label>TABLE 5</label><caption><p>The Hosmer-Lemeshow Test for Testing the Goodness of Fit of Models</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Model name</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">&#x003c7;&#x000b2;</th><th align="center" colspan="1" axis="1" rowspan="1">Task</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.095</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">20.028</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.651</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">10.517</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.176</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">17.532</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.701</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">9.908</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.620</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">30.436</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">AP-HBP-Hab-Rad</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.756</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">16.478</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Combined</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.137</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">30.436</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Train</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Combined</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">0.834</td><td align="char" char="." colspan="1" rowspan="1">16.477</td><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Validation</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values&#x0003e;0.05 indicate the goodness of fit of the models.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>AFP indicates alpha-fetoprotein; AP-Hab-Rad, arterial phase habitat radiomic signature; AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, the integration of arterial phase and hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature; combined, combining the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model with age and AFP; HBP-Hab-Rad, hepatobiliary phase habitat radiomic signature.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>FIGURE 8</label><caption><p>Decision curve analysis (DCA) curves. The <italic toggle="yes">y</italic>-axis represents the net benefit, while the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis displays the threshold probability, which is the point where the expected benefit of treatment equals the expected benefit of not receiving treatment. Training cohort (A) and validation cohort (B). The combined model and the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model show a net benefit for patients across most threshold probabilities.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rct-49-880-g009" position="float"/></fig></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>In this study, we predicted the proliferative HCC using habitat-derived radiomics based on Gd-EOB-DTPA intratumorally heterogeneity. Comparing the AP and HBP habitat radiomics model (LR model), the models for combined AP and HBP habitat radiomics (Naive Bayes model) showed superior performance. Then, the combined predictive model incorporating the AP and HBP habitat radiomics, age, and AFP for predicting the proliferation of HCC with optimal predictive performance. For the combined model, the training cohort had an AUC value of 0.936 (95% CI: 0.897-0.974), and the validation cohort had an AUC value of 0.886 (95% CI: 0.784-0.988). Although the AP-HBP-Hab-Rad model had the highest performance in the training cohort with an AUC value of 0.966 (95% CI: 0.937-0.994), the AUC value in the validation cohort was 0.877 (95% CI: 0.786-0.969), which is inferior to the combined mode in the validation cohort with an AUC value of 0.886 (95% CI: 0.784-0.988). The difference in AUC was statistically significant. So, the combined predictive model incorporating the AP and HBP habitat radiomics, age, and AFP was the optimal model for predicting the proliferation of HCC.</p><p>To maximize the utilization of clinical factors, we select <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.3 as the cutoff value to include clinical factors. Then, the clinically relevant risk factors identified for predicting proliferative HCC were serum AFP and age. According to previous studies,<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>,<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref></sup> the proliferative HCC was associated with higher serum AFP levels, consistent with our findings. Previous studies have used imaging features and clinicopathologic variables to predict the proliferative of HCC.<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup> According to Heo et al,<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup> Kang et al,<sup><xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref></sup> and Bao et al<sup><xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup> studies, rim AP hyperenhancement, LR-M, lobulated shape, mosaic architecture, and satellite lesions may help to identify proliferative class HCC, which showed greater correlations with more tumor rapid growth. Gd-EOB-DTPA enhanced MRI quantitative parameter (the lesion-to-liver contrast enhancement ratio) in the HBP can also predict the proliferative subtype of HCC, with its area under the ROC curve of 0.812 (95% CI: 0.748-0.877).<sup><xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref></sup> Recently, Wang et al<sup><xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref></sup> have constructed a CT-based radiomics model to predict proliferative HCC preoperatively. The results showed that a radiomics nomogram incorporating the fusion radiomics model using all 4 phases of CT images and the clinical factors could predict proliferative HCC. The radiomics nomogram showed a superior predictive capability with AUCs (internal test: 0.87; external test: 0.85). In contrast to previous studies, we chose the radiomics features of Gd-EOB-DTPA Enhanced MRI that reflect the texture characteristics of each subregion and achieved a satisfactory performance. This high performance in our study could be explained by the fact that the Gd-EOB-DTPA-enhanced MRI was superior to CT for diagnosing HCC,<sup><xref rid="R23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref></sup> which was associated with the molecular and pathologic factors of HCCs.<sup><xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> In particular, we utilized the subregions analysis of Gd-EOB-DTPA enhanced MRI for predicting proliferative HCC. Different subregions have unique structural, metabolic, and functional characteristics even in the same HCC. Introducing the concept of subregions/habitats-derived radiomics could more accurately reflect the tumor microenvironment, which is associated with tumor cellular proliferative status. We have incorporated the qualitative and quantitative information from Gd-EOB-DTPA enhanced MRI by habitat-derived radiomics and clinical data of patients with HCC, maximizing the exploration of clinical and imaging data for predicting proliferative HCC in our study.</p><p>In this study, we cohort the number of radiomic features to 5, all derived from the Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM). The decision to prioritize GLCM features was motivated by their high capacity to capture complex textural information, which is critical for quantifying intratumoral heterogeneity.<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref></sup> These features are Contrast, DifferenceEntropy, JointEnergy, JointEntropy, and Correlation. Contrast reflects the degree of local changes in the image; Entropy and Differential Entropy reflect its complexity and irregularity; JointEntropy and JointEnergy comprehensively reflect its uniformity and complexity; Correlation reflects the gray-level correlation within the image. In the field of medical imaging analysis, contrast, correlation, entropy, and energy are considered discriminative indicators that clearly reveal feature differences across images. This characteristic is reflected in breast cancer,<sup><xref rid="R25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref></sup> lung cancer,<sup><xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref></sup> prostate cancer,<sup><xref rid="R27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref></sup> and other studies. Moreover, JointEnergy and Correlation have low computational complexity, making them suitable for processing high-resolution medical images. Selecting fewer but representative texture features improves computational efficiency without compromising analysis quality. In summary, the 5 features of Contrast, Differential Entropy, JointEnergy, JointEntropy, and Correlation were selected for local feature extraction due to their aforementioned advantages. Unlike first-order features, which only analyze pixel intensity and GLRLM, GLSZM, and NGTDM features, which explore runs, zones, and neighbor properties at a macro level, GLCM offers finer granularity by analyzing spatial relationships between pixels at an intensity level. This makes it particularly suited for intratumoral analysis.</p><p>Proliferative HCCs are a class of aggressive tumors with poor prognosis, which is associated with TP53 mutations chromosomal instability.<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>,<xref rid="R28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref></sup> Proliferative HCCs usually lead to a higher recurrence rate and worse overall survival after curative partial hepatectomy or transarterial chemoembolization (TACE).<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>,<xref rid="R28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref></sup> Recently, studies have indicated that the proliferative HCCs may affect the therapeutic outcomes of microwave ablation and targeted therapy or immunotherapy.<sup><xref rid="R29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>,<xref rid="R30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref></sup> Generally, the diagnosis of proliferative HCC is confirmed with histologic analysis of biopsy samples or postoperative specimens. However, pretreatment biopsies are not mandatory for HCC in clinical practice. The biopsy is invasive style, and the HCC is the only tumor that can be given a final diagnosis by noninvasive imaging in at-risk patients with cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis.<sup><xref rid="R31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref></sup> Hence, if we can determine the phenotype class of HCCs before treatment, we can stratify clinical outcomes to predict the tumor microenvironment and therapeutic outcomes, which could assist clinical doctors in making rational treatment decisions. However, the traditional radiologist's diagnostic mode of reading imaging features is time-consuming, labor-intensive, and has significant subjective differences. Therefore, using radiomics, especially habitat radiomics, to predict the phenotype class of HCCs before treatment is very important in clinical practice.</p><p>Our study had several limitations. First, the sample size was small, and the training and validation cohorts were from the same single institution, which would restrict the model&#x02019;s propagation. Second, there may have been unavoidable selection bias due to the retrospective nature and the included criteria. Third, the locations of the tumors were not explicitly factored into the analysis, which may affect the performance of the predictive radiomics model.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions"><title>CONCLUSIONS</title><p>A combined model based on enhanced MRI, including AP-HBP-Hab-Rad, serum AFP, and age using the LightGBM algorithm, can satisfactorily preoperatively predict proliferative HCC.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn fn-type="other"><p>S.S.: wrote the initial draft of the manuscript. Y.F. and Z.J.: guaranteed the integrity of the entire study. S.S., Y.Y., S.X., and Q.H.: contributed to the study concepts and design. S.S. and Y.Y.: contributed to revise the manuscript. Y.F. and S.S. contributed to segmenting tumors with software. S.S. and Y.Y.: acquire, analyze, and interpret data. S.S. and Y.Y.: contributed equally to this work and should be considered cofirst authors.</p></fn><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p>The authors declare no conflict of interest.</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="R1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal">
